  "body": "HTML parsers are used not just in user agents, but also in server-side applications, such as sanitizers.  As such, it is necessary to be able to parse HTML in linear time.\r\n\r\nHowever, it is not obvious from the specification that this is possible.  The specification calls for many O(n) lookups in various places, many of which could seemingly be triggered O(n) times.  As such, many HTML parsers may be vulnerable to algorithmic complexity denial of service attacks.  At the very least, it is highly non-obvious how to construct one that is not.  Since avoiding DoS attacks is not optional, this forces servers to use non-conforming parsers, potentially corrupting markup.  While this can be generally avoided by ensuring that all input is in a reasonably “canonical form” (all tags are complete and properly nested, etc – basically what an HTML serializer would generate from a conforming DOM), it is still highly suboptimal.  Finally, it creates a situation where using a proper HTML parser creates a security vulnerability(!) – obviously a bad thing.\r\n\r\nOne solution would be to impose a cap on the length of the stack of open elements and the list of active formatting elements.  Alternatively, the specification could be changed to restate the algorithm in terms of flags, which are maintained during parsing and are O(1) to check.",
